From: DEREK@alcor.org
Date:  Tue, 27 Sep 94 18:59:50 
Subject: [#94-9-562] Cryonics and Metabolic Dependent State Loss

John M. Bozeman writes:

>I admit that I am not a professional biologist, and most of my
>grad bio. classes were in botany (oops!)... but I question the 
>"technological limitations" argument--at least in part.  If they
>can do the lowering of body temperature experiment with dogs,
>why not with humans?   Are humans that much more fragile?   

You are talking about two different procedures.  Dogs have been 
cooled to just above freezing and held at that temperature for 
upwards of five hours.  This is not cryonic suspension, it is 
hypothermic total body washout.  (They perfuse the animals with a 
substance similar to the organ preservation solutions used to keep 
kidneys intended for transplant viable outside of the body for up to 
five days at similar temperatures.)  The reason for the five hour 
limit is that chemical processes are not completely stopped, just 
drastically slowed.  Irreversible toxic reactions do eventually occur.  

In theory, there is no reason that this technology could not be 
adapted for humans.  In fact, hypothermia is already being used to a 
limited extent when surgeons need extra time for delicate surgery.  
But in these cases, I don't think the patient is ever cooled below 20 
degrees C.

*Freezing* a mammal of any kind is a completely different story.  
Water (i.e., the main ingredient in us) crystallizes, causing 
ultrastructural fracturing, among other things.  It is indeed a 
"technological limitation" which we have yet to overcome.

>And, to take a different direction, what about the combination of
>cooling of the body... but not necessarily freezing it solid...
>in conjuction with, say Zombie Toxin or something similar?

Well, I don't know what "Zombie Toxin" is, but if you replace it 
with "Glycerol" or some other cryoprotectant, you've described 
something very much like the vitrification approach, which has 
indeed been tried in many different variations.  (Sorry to spoil your 
copyright fun.  :-)  If we could get the cells and tissues to absorb 
enough cryoprotectant, we could prevent water crystallization, and 
just supercool everything to the point of metabolic arrest.  We can 
do this successfully with many kinds of cells, and some kinds of 
tissue. Cryobiologists are even very close (say, six months to five 
years, depending on who you ask) to vitrifying a whole kidney.

But vitrifying the brain seems to be even further off, and whether 
we will successfully vitrify and thaw a whole mammal before we 
have full blown nanotech (and thus, other ways of solving the 
problem) is a question I've not yet heard a definitive answer for.

Forward in all directions!

Derek Ryan
derek@alcor.org

